{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>To the goat-demons (לַשְּׂעִירִים)</b> — to the demons (שֵׁדִים). And thus it says (Isaiah 13:21), \"And goat-demons (וּשְׂעִירִים) shall dance there\" — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 17:7), from Torat Kohanim (chapter 9:8). And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 17:7) said that the demons are so called (שֵׁדִים) because the body that sees them is shattered/terrified (מִתְיַשֵּׁש/מִשְׁתַּעֵר). But the nearer [explanation] is that they are so called because those who are mad see them in the likeness of goats (שְׂעִירִים). And the word \"still\" (עוֹד) teaches that thus Israel would do in Egypt. <b>after whom they go astray (אֲשֶׁר הֵם זֹנִים)</b> — for everyone who seeks them and believes in them goes astray from beneath his Elohim, because he thinks that there is someone who does good or harm apart from HaShem, the honored and awesome One. And Rabbi Abraham already hinted at the truth by means of the word \"still\" (עוֹד), as I wrote above (Ramban on Leviticus 16:8). And the name \"goat-demons\" (שְׂעִירִים) will become clear to you from there. And they are called \"demons\" (שֵׁדִים) because their dwelling is in a desolate place (מָקוֹם שָׁדוּד), such as the wilderness, and their principal existence is in the extremities, such as the desolate northern region because of the cold. And know that just as creation at the beginning, in the act of Genesis, began with the body of man and with all living creatures and plants and minerals from the four elements, and all four combined by divine power so that from them would come a coarse body perceptible to all five senses by virtue of its thickness and coarseness — so too there was a formation from two elements, from fire and air, and from them came a body not perceptible and not grasped by any of the senses, just as the soul of an animal is not apprehended by the senses because of its subtlety. And this body is spiritual; it flies by virtue of its subtlety and lightness in fire and air. And just as in everything the cause of coming-to-be and passing-away in what is compounded from four elements lies in the combination, so too it is with these compounded from two elements, for when they are joined the possessor of the body is alive, and when they separate he is like a dead man. And therefore our Sages said (Ḥagigah 16a) that six things were stated concerning demons: three like the ministering angels and three like human beings. Three like the ministering angels — they have wings like the ministering angels, and they fly like the ministering angels, and they know what is about to come like the ministering angels — \"they know,\" you might say? Rather, say: they hear what is about to be. Three like human beings — they eat and drink like human beings, they propagate and multiply like human beings, and they die like human beings. And the cause of death is the separation of the combination, which is the cause in all compounded beings, and the cause of their flight is the lightness of their elements, as is seen also in birds: because fire and air predominate in them and the other elements in them are scant, it flies and soars — how much more so these, in which there are none of the heavy elements at all, whose flight is great; they fly without wearying. And the nature of their eating is to lick from the waters and the fire the vapors and the moistures, like the manner of fire that licks the water that is in the trench (I Kings 18:38). And this is the matter of the incense that the practitioners of necromancy (בַּעֲלֵי נגרומנסיא\"ה) make for the demons, and its cause is the dryness that draws the fire that is in the air into their body, and it needs to be restored — just as eating in man serves to replace what is consumed from him. And the meaning of what they said, that they \"hear what is about to be,\" is that in their flight through the air of the heavens they receive what is future from the officers of the constellations that dwell in the air, and they are the governors of the [lower] world, and from there also the winged creatures announce what is future, as is experienced with serpents and as I will explain in its place (Deuteronomy 18:9), with God's help. And both these and those do not know what is to come in many days and distant times; rather, they make known only what is about to happen soon, for we know from experiments of theirs that they receive from the demons what is decreed to come. And therefore Onkelos translates (Onkelos on Leviticus 17:7): \"They sacrificed to demons who are not God (לֹא אֱלֹהַ)\" (Deuteronomy 32:17) — \"דַּבַּחוּ לְשֵׁדִין דְּלֵית בְּהוֹן צְרוֹךְ\" (they sacrificed to demons in whom there is no need) — he says that there is in them no need whatsoever, for they do not avert future harm nor do they produce any benefit, nor do they make known what is distant so that a person might preserve himself from it with understanding. And this is also the language of Scripture, \"not God (לֹא אֱלוֹהַ),\" as if it said \"no power or dominion,\" for Elohim is a term denoting might and strength — \"not El\" means that there is in the demons no might or strength whatsoever, and therefore there is no need for them, for they cannot do harm, nor can they do good."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "Ramban first cites Ibn Ezra's etymology that demons (שֵׁדִים) are so called because the body is terrified (מִשְׁתַּעֵר) upon seeing them; the reading מִתְיַשֵּׁש appears in some witnesses but the sense is the same. נגרומנסיא\"ה is a transliteration of nigromancia (necromancy) in medieval Latin/ Romance."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:12.799371816Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}